## Architectural Choices

### Why a tiny Rust HTTP server, not nginx

The user explicitly wants a tiny Rust server. The audio server in this plan
is single-purpose and stays under ~600 LOC of Rust. It uses `axum` over
`hyper`, supports byte-range requests via `tokio::fs`, and reverse-proxies
anything that isn't an audio path back to Flask. This means the Rust server
becomes the public container entrypoint port, and Flask binds to a private
loopback port.

### Why Flask still owns audio auth

Flask owns the user/session model, the Stripe billing checks, the whitelist
logic, and the download-counter writer call. Reproducing all of that in
Rust would balloon Phase 1 well past its budget. Instead we use the
nginx-style **auth subrequest** pattern: Rust asks Flask "may I serve this
file?" via an internal endpoint; Flask returns either a 4xx or a JSON blob
with `file_path`, `mtime`, `size`, `etag`, `mimetype`,
`content_disposition`, and `should_increment_download_count`. Rust then
streams the file and, if the flag is set, asynchronously POSTs back to the
counter increment endpoint after the stream completes.

### Why the Rust jobs runner does not write SQLite directly

We just spent effort centralizing all writes through the writer service.
Bypassing it from Rust would re-introduce two-writer SQLite contention
risks (database-is-locked errors, lost ordering, SQLAlchemy identity-map
staleness, missed event listeners). The Rust runner instead **calls the
existing writer IPC** for any mutation. The win is not "skip the writer" —
it is "stop running the polling loop in a heavyweight Python process that
holds an SQLAlchemy session, and stop allocating per-poll IPC queues."

